A||||7||Dale||El. trunk release, could it be this simple?||||11
Z||000000||Dale||09-23-1999||01:33 AM||||Well,  My 84 SE has the solonoid in the trunk.  It has the wiring in the dash.  It looks like all I have to do is buy a switch.  There has to be a catch.  Is it this simple?||||reg||11
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||09-23-1999||04:41 AM||||I'm a little confused, since I'm not aware of any Fiero <B>not</B> having that electronic release switch. So I would say: yes, it's that easy since that switch should've been there in the first place...||||reg||5
Z||000002||mwbackus||09-23-1999||07:02 AM||||My Formula came stock without the electric hatch release.  The wiring was there, cept for the run up to the hatch from the engine bay harness, but this was quite easy to change. I installed the relay, switch, and selenoid, and now I have a brand new hatch release.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||09-23-1999||09:18 AM||||I did this one, and yes it is that simple.......  All you have to do is find the dash switch, and the electric lock actuator.  But If you already have the lock gadget, all you need is the switch.   Ten Minutes to an electric trunk release!||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-23-1999||10:11 AM||||read here. <A HREF="http://home.sprynet.com/~theogre/fiero/pfindex.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.sprynet.com/~theogre/fiero/pfindex.htm</A> <P>I wrote it up a while back, we haven't gotten time to put it up into it's final home here. Yes. It's that easy. Yes, allot of Fiero came w/o the power release. Yes, all of them from 85 &up are very easy. Yes, you can rob the parts off any salvage yard car.<P>There are also some ideas how to make this option safer and more secure. If you have children around please do this. Every year a number of children die because they where trapped in car trunks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||09-23-1999||01:04 PM||||Damn.. I know the fiero only has two seats, and this is inconvienent for larger families, but what kind of parent would actually make his kids ride back there!  :-)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dale||09-23-1999||02:13 PM||||Only on long family trips  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">(this makes more room for luggage inside the car)<P>What I couldn't believe is that everything is all there except the switch.  Well since we don't have to many junk yards (none actualy) on this rock, is GM the only place to get the switch.  I will have to see how many arms and legs I have to spare. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-23-1999||03:29 PM||||dale, what year is that.....<P>on 85+ cars where the ajar switch is on the latch, there should be 4 wires feeding from the weather pack connector near the right hinge. Is the wire actually connected to the solinoid. There also needs to be a relay under the dash. it usually directly under the switch on the dash. it may be clipped to the side of the steering column supports.<P>If the wire isn't connected to the solinoid it's likely someone simply was replacing the latch with whatever they had.<P>Acording to my information..... the switch bezels are no longer available. You could modify one if your patient.||||reg||1
